  <section>
    <h2>Why board category matters more than weight</h2>
    <p>Roadside scrap dealers typically price PCBs by weight — a flat per-kg number regardless of what's on the board. That's convenient for them and unfair to you when your pile includes high-value boards mixed with generic ones.</p>
    <p>A category-aware quote splits the pile:</p>
    <p>Server and workstation motherboards carry dense, high-quality connectors and more layers of copper. They price at the top tier.</p>
    <p>Desktop and laptop motherboards are the workhorse category — most homes and small offices dispose of these. Mid-tier value; CPU socket condition and board age matter.</p>
    <p>RAM sticks and GPU cards trade as complete components, not raw PCBs. If they still work, they may qualify for a resale route (higher value) instead of material recovery.</p>
    <p>Generic consumer PCBs (from old routers, TVs, appliances, chargers) are lower value per kg but often make up bulk. Still worth including in the pickup.</p>
    <p>Ask for a category-broken quote if you're bringing a mixed batch.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What condition actually changes the number</h2>
    <p>For high-value boards, condition changes the estimate significantly:</p>
    <p>Complete boards vs stripped — a motherboard with the CPU socket, capacitors, and connectors intact is worth more than a stripped shell. If you've already pulled the CPU, mention it — the quote adjusts.</p>
    <p>Water damage, corrosion, burnt spots — visible damage typically drops the quote to the material-recovery baseline. Not a dealbreaker, just an adjustment.</p>
    <p>Age — a modern server board (recent generation) is worth more than a twenty-year-old vintage board with obsolete connectors, even if both weigh the same.</p>
    <p>Quantity — larger batches often price slightly better because handling and transport per-kg drop. If you have 5 kg+ of PCBs, mention the volume.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Skip the roadside scrap dealer route for PCBs</h2>
    <p>Roadside scrap dealers exist in Kochi and buy PCBs — but they typically:</p>
    <p>Quote a flat per-kg rate that lumps high-value boards with generic ones — you lose the premium for good motherboards.</p>
    <p>Don't do certified material recovery — the actual recycling stream is opaque, which matters if you want compliance documentation.</p>
    <p>Don't provide any documentation — no pickup acknowledgement, no invoice, no trace. Fine for a one-off sale, poor for a business or repeat volume.</p>
    <p>For occasional home clearouts, either route works. For repeat volume — computer shops, IT service providers, refurbishers, offices with recurring hardware refresh — go with a category-aware authorised recycler for both the better rate and the documentation trail.</p>
  </section>

